        type: "p",
        text: "One of the last places in India where ‘Kristi’ – a distinct mix of Portuguese Creole and local Marathi – is still spoken, Korlai is a tranquil stretch of coastline that presents a refreshing escape from Maharashtra’s more bustling seaside destinations. With its clean sands, clear waters, and unhurried pace, the beach invites visitors to slow down and savour the simple pleasures of a monsoon break by the sea. Apart from the beach, there’s Korlai Fort, perched atop a windswept hill 300 feet above the Arabian Sea, on the Island of Morro de Chaul. Standing silent and sentinel over the Revdanda Creek – built by the Portuguese in 1521 to guard this strategic waterway – the fort is surrounded by the sea on three sides, and offers stunning views. Meanwhile, the picturesque Korlai fishing village below, all colourful houses and quaint street-corner chapels, is equally alluring, just waiting to be explored.",
